---
import ScoutAggressiveHulls from "../../../components/studies/ScoutAggressiveHulls.astro";
import ScoutJoinedStern from "../../../components/studies/ScoutJoinedStern.astro";
import ScoutPackingComparison from "../../../components/studies/ScoutPackingComparison.astro";
import ScoutSplitKnifeVariants from "../../../components/studies/ScoutSplitKnifeVariants.astro";
import ScoutTridentPlacement from "../../../components/studies/ScoutTridentPlacement.svelte";
import ScoutWholeShipConcepts from "../../../components/studies/ScoutWholeShipConcepts.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "scout-firefly");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="purpose">
		<h2 id="purpose">01 / Small, cramped, and still angry</h2>
		<p>
			The Scout should return alongside the Fighter and Heavy as another angry
			triangle, but not a scaled-down Fighter. The Fighter has a long, faceted
			Lance silhouette; the Heavy stretches its hull around a larger complement
			of fittings. The Scout’s opportunity is compression: a slightly smaller
			craft whose cockpit, engines, and equipment seem to compete for every
			available bit of hull. More firefly than paper plane: tightly packed, not
			a literal insect silhouette.
		</p>
		<p>
			Imagine fitting the Scout’s single deck slot and single aft slot while its
			required side-engine pair is already installed. Even fully loaded, it
			should remain one small, purposeful spacecraft—not a loose collection of
			parts or a broad wing with equipment sprinkled over it. The aft slot will
			likely need a tail: a way to make room for that fitting without spreading
			the main body. Treat this as the leading placement hypothesis, not a
			settled tail shape. A round cockpit could give the crowded body a focal
			point against the sharp triangular hull; its shape also remains open.
		</p>
		<p>
			This cycle should settle how hull, cockpit, and cramped fittings make that
			identity readable together, both enlarged in the builder and at flight
			size. Keep the existing slot complement; leave numerical handling balance
			and new mechanics outside this visual decision. The tension to investigate
			is how tightly we can pack the ship without losing its silhouette or the
			ability to distinguish its fittings.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>The direction is a tightly packed machine, with a likely tail-mounted
				aft slot.</strong
			>
			The first comparison in 02 should examine how that tail can carry the
			fitting while keeping the whole ship compact and angry, rather than
			stretching it into another long Fighter.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration">
		<h2 id="exploration">02 / Packing before hull refinement</h2>
		<h3>02.1 / Cockpit first or deck first?</h3>
		<p>
			Both sketches use one compact triangular body and a short tail carrying
			the aft fitting. Only the round cockpit and deck slot exchange positions.
			A gives the ship a leading cockpit and crowded equipment behind it; B lets
			the deck fitting lead and nestles the pilot among the engines. Hide the
			mounts to inspect the underlying silhouette.
		</p>
		<ScoutPackingComparison />
		<p>
			E = required mirrored side engines · H = gun/utility · A =
			engine/gun/utility. Footprints come from the current Scout’s production
			mounts: 8 across × 10 fore–aft units. Counts and fitting identities are
			real; positions, 26° side angles, round cockpit and hull are study
			fixtures. The envelope is 59 units long and 34 wide, versus the Fighter’s
			73 × 40, shown at equal scale below the alternatives. The tail is included
			in that length; this is not a size comparison distorted by different zoom
			levels.
		</p>
		<p>
			The flat palette is shared with the Fighter; these are spatial sketches,
			not finished facets. Dashed guides show rearward engine axes, not plume
			widths. Empty footprints cannot establish installed-part clearance,
			flight-scale readability, collision or handling. Those remain later
			checks, after the arrangement gives us a useful hull direction.
		</p>
		<p>
			Owner direction: carry A’s leading cockpit forward. The cockpit may
			protrude beyond the hull, and the assembly should be more cramped. The
			first comparison remains unchanged above; B is not carried forward.
		</p>
		<h3 id="six-compact-hulls">
			02.2 / Six razor bodies around the light-bulb cockpit
		</h3>
		<p>
			The protruding light-bulb cockpit works; the previous bodies do not. This
			revision keeps that cockpit and replaces the blunt shoulders and square
			tails with long flat facets, sharp swept edges and pointed sterns. Spikes
			should grow out of the hull’s planes, not look like decorations attached
			to a rounded body. The original six are
			<a href="/lab/studies/archive/scout-firefly-rounded-bodies"
				>preserved separately</a
			>.
		</p>
		<ScoutAggressiveHulls />
		<p>
			01 and 04 test swept rearward blades; 02 tests forward fangs. 03 is the
			cleanest counter-triangle, with 05 adding barbed shoulders and 06
			narrowing the idea into a kite and diamond. Hide the footprints to judge
			those edges. All six now hold the cockpit and all four mount positions
			identical: radius 6 at (0,−21), H at (0,−7), E at (±10.5,2), A at (0,17),
			side angles ±26°. Production Scout footprints remain 8 × 10 units. Every
			card uses the same drawing scale; only hull and tail geometry change.
		</p>
		<p>
			These remain study-only flat-surface sketches using the Fighter palette.
			The protruding cockpit tests silhouette, not a new cockpit mechanic.
			Installed parts, attachment details, exhaust clearance and actual flight
			size still need review; empty boxes do not establish that every loadout
			fits.
		</p>
		<p>
			Owner review: 04 / Split knives is close, but its diamond tail feels
			wrong. Carry the split knife body into a focused comparison of tail-less
			sterns, alternative tails and heavier wings. This chooses a basis for
			refinement, not a final hull.
		</p>
		<h3 id="split-knife-variations">
			02.3 / Six ways to finish the Split knives
		</h3>
		<p>
			Keep the light-bulb cockpit and the deep cuts that separate the body from
			its swept blades. 01 removes the separate tail; 02 replaces the diamond
			with a clipped stinger; 03 tries a shorter counter-triangle. 04 and 05
			give the wings more armor, with and without a tail. 06 makes the blades
			longer than the central stern, so the knives—not a hanging tail—finish the
			silhouette.
		</p>
		<ScoutSplitKnifeVariants />
		<p>
			All six use the same scale, radius-6 cockpit, deck position and mirrored
			side-engine positions/angles from 02.2. Mount footprints remain 8 × 10.
			“No tail” means no separate appendage, not removal of the aft slot: A
			moves into an integral stern. Its fore–aft position varies from 12 to 15
			units to match its support; H and the engine pair do not move. These are
			study-only drawings, not simulated or fitted production ships.
		</p>
		<p>
			Hide the footprints to compare the wing weight and stern shapes, then show
			them to inspect where A sits. A fitting footprint is not its complete part
			or plume: barrel, nozzle and exhaust clearance, attachment details and
			flight-size readability remain unverified. The original Split knives and
			its diamond tail remain above as the reference.
		</p>
		<p>
			Owner review: all six are worse than the original 02.2 / 04 Split knives.
			None is carried forward. Exploration is paused to recover the intended
			body character; the original remains the closest reference, not an
			accepted final design. The light-bulb cockpit remains settled.
		</p>
		<p>
			The original’s swept outline, proportions and feeling of speed are the
			qualities to preserve, confirmed in discussion. The light-bulb cockpit
			also stays. The failed variations treated wings and tails as
			interchangeable appendages instead of preserving that coherent, fast
			silhouette. The owner confirmed the tail problem: it reads as a separate
			ornament hanging behind the ship. The next comparison should preserve the
			original’s outline and proportions while testing a more integrated stern.
		</p>
		<h3 id="joined-stern">02.4 / One hull, not a pendant</h3>
		<p>
			A preserves the original 02.2 / 04 exactly. B fills out only the pinched
			upper tail so it meets the central body at full width. The cockpit, wing
			outline, widest part of the tail, tail tip and all mount positions are
			identical. This isolates the attachment problem rather than redesigning
			the whole ship again. Hide the mount footprints to judge the connection.
		</p>
		<ScoutJoinedStern />
		<p>
			Same-scale study sketches using the original palette and Scout mount
			footprints. This tests visual continuity only; neither version establishes
			installed-part or exhaust clearance, collision, or flight handling. B is a
			hypothesis, not an accepted replacement.
		</p>
		<p>
			This comparison did not advance the study: changing the neck was an
			irrelevant detail, not meaningful ideation. The owner clarified that the
			answer is unknown and the study should help discover it, not require a
			sketch of a solution or refine a prematurely fixed hull.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Reframe the next inquiry around distinct whole-ship concepts:</strong
			>
			how can a tightly packed Scout combine the light-bulb cockpit with a
			slick, swept, aggressive body that feels fast? Original Split knives
			supplies promising qualities, not frozen vertices, wing dimensions or
			mount coordinates. Body, wings and stern may be conceived together; the
			aft slot remains required, but a separate tail is not mandatory. Preserve
			the cockpit character, compactness and four-slot complement while
			exploring materially different spatial ideas. The original remains a
			reference; the later refinements establish no accepted hull direction.
		</p>
		<h3 id="whole-ship-ideas">
			02.5 / Six whole-ship ideas, not six tail edits
		</h3>
		<p>
			Each sketch starts from a different way to organize the whole spacecraft:
			a solid delta, a swept crescent, twin runners, a forward-loaded dart, a
			barbed lifting body or an integrated trident. The light-bulb cockpit,
			compact packing and four slots remain. Body, wing, stern and equipment
			placement are open together. Split knives contributes sweep and speed, not
			a frozen outline to decorate.
		</p>
		<ScoutWholeShipConcepts />
		<p>
			All six use the same scale, radius-6 cockpit and the production Scout’s 8
			× 10 mount footprints. E is the mirrored side pair, H the deck fitting and
			A the aft fitting—even when there is no separate tail. Mount positions and
			side angles are proposals specific to each concept. The wider crescent
			also tests whether width can convey sweep without making the ship feel
			large. These are original flat-polygon study sketches using the Fighter’s
			palette, not production rendering or simulated flight. Fitted-part and
			exhaust clearance, attachment details and handling remain untested.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Which ideas contain something worth pursuing, and what is it?</strong
			>
			There need not be one winner: we can carry a useful spatial idea from one
			and a sense of speed from another. Hide the footprints to inspect the
			whole silhouettes, then show them to judge the packing.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Owner acceptance: 02.5 / 06 Integrated trident.</strong>
			The hull direction is approved: retain its exact flat facets, swept outer
			knives, continuous pointed central keel and protruding round cockpit. Slot
			positions and side angles are explicitly the next investigation, not
			settled by that approval. Acceptance is not production integration.
		</p>
		<h3 id="trident-placement">02.6 / Fit the accepted trident</h3>
		<p>
			The enlarged hull below consumes the exact same reference geometry as 02.5
			/ 06. Select the mirrored side pair, H or A, then move it with the
			sliders; arrow keys work too. Side position, separation and angle change
			together for the pair. H and A can move independently, including across
			the centerline. Reset restores the original sketch’s coordinates.
		</p>
		<ScoutTridentPlacement client:load />
		<p>
			Start by inspecting the side pair: does it sit on the wing roots, and do
			its rearward axes point clear of the outer knives? Then check H’s
			separation from the cockpit and A’s support on the central keel. The
			original sketch values were H (0,−7), A (0,11), sides (±11,0) at ±26°.
			Reset preserves those hypotheses for comparison; the accepted screenshot
			layout below is the workbench’s default.
		</p>
		<p>
			This is a spatial workbench, not a flight trial. Nozzle guides reuse the
			production extension, but engine surfaces, wide plumes and gun barrels are
			not drawn. Slot overlap, hull overhang and nozzle/hull intersections are
			allowed so problems remain visible; there is no automatic clearance
			verdict. Position and angle will affect force and steering leverage in
			production, so visual approval alone cannot settle handling. Use the
			coordinate table or copyable JSON to share a candidate placement.
		</p>
		<p>
			Screenshot candidate: the owner supplied a trial with the side pair at
			(±6,−10.5), angles ±49°. Those values are visible and exact. H at
			approximately (0,−1) and A at approximately (0,11.5) are reconstructed
			from the image, not measured coordinates. “Load accepted screenshot
			layout” preserves this placement alongside the original reset point. The
			workbench now opens on that accepted layout.
			<a href="/study-references/scout-trident-placement.png"
				>Inspect the supplied screenshot.</a
			>
			The owner subsequently approved keeping this layout and requested parts 03
			and 04. This settles the spatial direction, not flight clearance or exact
			H/A measurements.
		</p>
		<p>
			The forward pair now sits around the cockpit base, with the engine axes
			diverging sharply away from the wings. At this tight spacing, the inner
			mount corners overlap slightly, and the outer forward corners sit near the
			cockpit collar. Those are relationships to check with installed parts; the
			aft engine guide still runs over the keel tip.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Accepted reference:</strong>
			exact 02.5 / 06 hull and cockpit, with the 02.6 supplied screenshot’s
			forward side pair and compact axial H/A arrangement. Preserve the swept
			outline, proportions, flat facets, feeling of speed and cramped cockpit
			cluster. Installed-part fidelity and handling remain human-review
			questions, not claims of this study.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">
			03 / Feasible as a shared Scout alpha, with fitting risks
		</h2>
		<p>
			The existing Scout already has the required mirrored engine pair, one deck
			slot, one aft slot, mass 60 and version-1 saved builds. The shared
			builder, production Canvas renderer, mounted plumes, energy model and
			flight simulation are reusable. At this readiness assessment, Scout
			selection was disabled and production still drew its generic hull. The
			adaptations below describe what the delivery needed; the implemented
			result is recorded at the end of 04. No dependency, backend or new
			gameplay mechanic was needed for the alpha trial.
		</p>
		<h3>Adaptations required for a meaningful trial</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Shared Scout geometry.</strong>
				Promote the exact accepted polygons, palette, black circular cockpit and
				collar into a typed mode-a definition. Give the Scout explicit mounts
				consumed by drawing, builder hit targets and nozzle emitters. Do not
				substitute the rejected diamond-tail designs or silently enlarge the 8 ×
				10 footprints.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Actual fitting conflicts.</strong>
				The inner side footprints slightly overlap at ±6 and 49°, and approach
				the cockpit collar. The pre-integration Scout rail barrel extended 18
				units forward: at H near −1 it can cross the cockpit; from A near 11.5
				it can cross H. These are concrete risks from the production renderer,
				not cleared by empty boxes. Inspect every compatible fitting and the
				paired selection targets. A fidelity review is required before calling
				the delivered assembly resolved. If accommodation requires changing the
				accepted silhouette or spatial relationships, return that bounded
				conflict for review instead of silently moving the mounts.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Nozzle and plume treatment.</strong>
				The aft nozzle at about 19.5 lies over the keel that ends at 29.
				Establish how a deck-mounted engine and its exhaust read over that
				surface; inspect the forward pair’s full plumes near the wings. An
				explicit surface-mount treatment may suffice; cutting a channel or
				changing the keel is not already approved. Axis guides alone do not
				establish clearance.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Coordinate and response connection.</strong>
				Convert study (across, aft) to production (−aft, across). Set sideX
				10.5, arm 6, angle 49°, with opposite left/right signs. Existing
				force/turning calculations consume these values; the signed lever 6
				cos(49°) + 10.5 sin(49°) is positive, so the existing steering direction
				can be retained. This is not a handling approval.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Collision coverage.</strong>
				The pre-integration Scout returned a fixed 16-unit radius. Replace that
				approximation with a conservative envelope including the new hull,
				circular cockpit/collar, mounts, nozzles and relevant fitted-part
				extents at the existing 0.65 drawing scale. A circle overestimates the
				empty spaces between the knives; collision fairness remains a
				flight-review limitation.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Blockers versus provisional alpha values</h3>
		<p>
			Shared geometry, correct mounts/nozzles, collision coverage and re-enabled
			selection are prerequisites for testing this design at all. Actual part
			and exhaust inspection is a prerequisite for claiming a faithful assembly,
			but can happen in the production-backed alpha rather than through another
			gallery. H −1 and A 11.5 are sufficient reconstructed starting values; the
			accepted relationships matter more than claiming screenshot precision.
		</p>
		<p>
			Keep mass 60, current engine thrust, energy draw and speed limits. Use
			gain 1 initially, not the Fighter’s unrelated 2.5× tuning. Document the
			rotational length proxy explicitly: the cockpit collar gives a 27.8-unit
			forward extent; the aft keel and wing tips must still contribute to
			collision independently. Length remains a handling approximation, not a
			physical moment of inertia. Turning responsiveness, barrel treatment,
			plume readability and circular-collision fairness need human review.
			Gravity, friction, scoring and shooting behavior remain unchanged.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="integration">
		<h2 id="integration">
			04 / Promote the accepted reference into builder and flight
		</h2>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Plan recorded before implementation; delivery was subsequently
				authorized by the owner’s “integrate it to the app, make it
				playable.”</strong
			>
			The accepted reference is 02.5 / 06 plus the approved 02.6 screenshot
			layout, preserved in
			<code>scout-trident-reference.ts</code>
			and the supplied image. The delivery record below distinguishes actual
			implementation from this preserved plan and from deployment.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Left/right side centers: study (−6,−10.5)/(6,−10.5), rotations
				+49°/−49°; production (10.5,−6)/(10.5,6), angles +49°/−49°.
			</li>
			<li>
				H: study (0,−1), production (1,0); A: study (0,11.5), production
				(−11.5,0). Both are axial, angle 0. Their fore–aft coordinates remain
				reconstructed implementation starting points.
			</li>
			<li>
				Footprints: study 8 across × 10 fore–aft, production 10 × 8. Cockpit:
				study (0,−21), black radius 6, collar radius 6.8. Preserve all six
				accepted hull planes and their flat colors.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Define shared geometry first.</strong>
				Add
				<code>packages/mode-a/src/lib/scout.ts</code>
				with converted polygons, cockpit/collar, explicit mounts and documented
				length convention. Keep the study’s original reference independent so
				later shared changes cannot erase the accepted evidence. Reuse existing
				Zod loadout/chassis contracts; if a data shape must change, update its
				schema before consumers. No save-schema change is currently needed.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Connect rendering and calculations.</strong>
				Update
				<code>build-renderer.ts</code>, <code>schemas/loadout.ts</code> and
				collision coverage. Make mounts, hit targets and plume emitters consume
				the same values. Reuse <code>build-flight.ts</code> and
				<code>simulation.ts</code>; change only assumptions that prevent this
				geometry from working. Keep the four-slot identities and indices.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Make the alpha inspectable.</strong>
				Re-enable Scout in
				<code>apps/web/src/components/ShipBuilder.svelte</code>
				and remove its pending-design notice. Preserve the
				<code>scout</code>
				ID,
				<code>void-drift-builds-v1</code>
				storage and independent Fighter/Heavy builds. Keep stock scored ships
				and custom unranked survival separate.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Review real assemblies before declaring fidelity.</strong>
				Use <a href="/lab/builder">/lab/builder</a> as the canonical shared
				renderer and test-flight destination, alongside <code>/builder</code>.
				Inspect empty mounts, both engine types, every H/A part, paired guns,
				cockpit separation, ambiguous hit targets and all-engine plumes. Keep
				the original hull unchanged while exposing any fitting conflict. Seek a
				narrow review if resolving a conflict would alter accepted geometry.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Test, document and hand back for play.</strong>
				Add Scout coverage to loadout/build-flight tests and browser builder
				tests: storage preservation, geometry and nozzle alignment, collision
				envelope, left/right signs, mirrored cancellation, single/paired/aft
				thrust, energy exhaustion and mixed-engine overspeed. Verify keyboard
				selection and existing landscape flight touch controls. Update
				<code>docs/design.md</code>
				and <code>docs/specs/ship-builder.md</code>
				with delivered behavior and known limits, then run
				<code>pnpm verify</code>
				and report each failed or unavailable gate. Human review separately
				assesses speed, cramped character, fitted readability and handling.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Sequence: shared Scout definition → mounts/renderer/collision and chassis
			calculations → selection and canonical Lab assembly → fitting/flight
			review and tests → authoritative documentation. Do not tune away the
			accepted silhouette to disguise a handling problem. Mark integrated only
			after actual implementation and verification; publishing needs separate
			authorization.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Implemented locally.</strong>
			The exact Trident hull and cockpit, approved side pair and reconstructed
			H/A coordinates are now shared by the app builder, canonical Lab and real
			flight systems. All three chassis are selectable; saved IDs and builds are
			preserved. Scout’s legacy long gun graphics now use the same
			mount-relative barrel treatment as Fighter and Heavy, clearing the cockpit
			without changing slots or silhouette. The aft engine is a deck/surface
			mount; no keel notch was added.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/builder?chassis=scout">Play the integrated Scout in the app</a>
			or
			<a href="/lab/builder?chassis=scout"
				>inspect and fly the same Scout in the Lab</a
			>. Targeted unit/browser checks exercised geometry, nozzles, collision,
			fittings, save preservation, keyboard flight, reset/return and landscape
			two-touch/cancel/blur. Full repository gate results are recorded
			separately in <code>artifacts/verification/latest.json</code>. Human
			fidelity, close-packed engine fit, plume/keel appearance and handling
			review remain pending. This is not a push, deployment or release claim.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/lab/reviews/scout-firefly">Separate study-quality review</a>
			records the ideation failures, recovery, acceptance and remaining evidence
			gaps. It is not this feasibility assessment or an implementation gate.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
code {
	overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
</style>
